Day 2 — Explore the solution space 
Research 3–5 existing tools, papers, datasets, or projects in the same space. Don't just bookmark — write 2–3 lines on what each does well and where it falls short. 
Map out 2–3 possible solution approaches at a high level. You're not committing yet; you're surveying. 
For each approach, list the underlying technical problems (data availability, model choice, API limits, infra cost, edge cases, etc.). 
Pick a primary approach and a backup. Justify the choice in writing — one short paragraph. 
Identify external dependencies: APIs, datasets, third-party services, paid tools. Flag anything that needs procurement or access early. 
Deliverable: A one-page "Project Discovery Note" (Markdown or Notion) covering problem, user, approach, risks, dependencies. Mentor sign-off required before moving to Stage 2.

Mapping Possible Alternate Solution Spaces
1. Low-Code Workflow 
Stitches together third-party data scraping nodes and AI APIs using visual automation engines like n8n or Make.com.
The Trade-off: Direct trade of engineering time for operational capital. You skip writing boilerplate API integration code but inherit high recurring platform transaction costs.
Critical Weaknesses: * Rigid Analytics: Utterly fails at executing custom mathematical trend clustering or complex, fine-grained volume spike algorithms.
Brittle Chains: If an upstream scraping node changes its data payload format by even a single key, the entire visual workflow breaks silently without robust error handling.
2. Browser Extension Overlay 
Shifts execution entirely to the browser, adding an AI-driven "Analyze for Advocacy" button directly into native Reddit or Instagram web interfaces.
The Trade-off: Eliminates all background server infrastructure and API scraping headaches in exchange for shifting the burden of initial trend discovery entirely onto human eyes.
Critical Weaknesses:
Zero Early Detection: Fails the primary project goal of algorithmically capturing emerging trends early, as it is completely blind to data until a human manually finds and clicks a post.
Fragmented Context: Cannot compute cross-platform deduplication or volume spikes because it only sees one browser page at a time.
3. Multi-Agent Conversational Workspace 
Replaces a rigid dashboard with a conversational interface driven by an autonomous framework (like LangGraph) coordinating specialized Scraper, Moderator, and Writer agents.
The Trade-off: Offers unmatched query flexibility and deep reasoning capabilities at the expense of massive processing latency and highly unpredictable token costs.
Critical Weaknesses:
Agent Drift & Loops: Autonomous agents are notoriously prone to getting stuck in execution loops or hallucinating API parameter inputs when left unattended.
The Speed Penalty: Parsing data through multiple layers of LLM reasoning takes time; what a database query can tell you in milliseconds takes an agent swarm 30 to 90 seconds to process.
4. Crowdsourced Bot
Replaces automated data scrapers entirely by letting a global network of trusted volunteers forward viral links directly to a centralized Telegram or Discord intake bot.
The Trade-off: Completely bypasses platform API restrictions, developer terms of service, and scraping blocks by leveraging decentralized human power, but trades technical complexity for community management overhead.
Critical Weaknesses:
Network Dependency: The pipeline's input data velocity lives and dies by the consistency, mood, and active participation of your volunteer base.
Blind Spots: Completely misses quiet, macro-algorithmic shifts or growing mainstream conversations that haven't hit an activist's personal feed yet.

